# Files that must never be served from the apex, listed defensively.
#
# Everything under dist/client is uploaded as a static asset, and dist/client is
# public/ plus the build's own output — so anything that lands there is a candidate
# for being served. Two things land there without being asked for:
#
#   .DS_Store       macOS writes it into public/, and the build copies it across.
#   .vite/          the build writes .vite/manifest.json — a map of every source
#                   module and chunk. Build metadata, not something to publish.
#
# HONEST ABOUT WHAT THIS IS: belt and braces, NOT a verified fix.
#
# `wrangler deploy --dry-run` reports "Read N files from the assets directory", and
# that number is unchanged by adding or removing this file — but it DROPS when the
# file itself is deleted from public/. So the count measures files read, before
# ignores are applied, and is no evidence about what actually gets uploaded. Whether
# these patterns take effect could not be established without a real deploy.
#
# The certain fix was deleting `public/.DS_Store` outright, which is done. It is
# gitignored, so it never reaches another machine or CI — but macOS recreates it
# locally, and the build copies whatever is in public/ at the time.
#
# So this file stays as the durable guard, and DEPLOY.md carries the check that
# settles it for real:
#     curl -I https://classkudos.com/.DS_Store        # expect 404
# If that ever returns 200, these patterns are not working — most likely an
# anchoring difference, since wrangler's own defaults use a leading slash
# (`/.assetsignore`), so try `/.DS_Store` instead.
#
# @cloudflare/vite-plugin PREPENDS this file to the generated
# dist/client/.assetsignore, which already covers `wrangler.json` and `.dev.vars`,
# and wrangler itself defaults to ignoring `/.assetsignore`, `/_headers` and
# `/_redirects`. Do not restate those.
.DS_Store
.vite/**
